<title>CHILE: Chile sees sharply lower copper earnings next year.</title>
<headline>Chile sees sharply lower copper earnings next year.</headline>
<dateline>SANTIAGO 1996-10-08</dateline>
<text>
<p>Lower copper prices will cut Chile's export income by about 10 percent next year over 1997, Finance Minister Eduardo Aninat said.</p>
<p>Aninat also said the contribution of copper exports to Chile's income next year will be over 10 percent less than this year. In a speech to Congress, he also reaffirmed the government's average copper price forecast of 94 cents a pound next year.</p>
<p>&quot;We are forecasting a drop in fiscal income from copper, to such degree that on the basis of an average copper price of 94 cents a pound, we can project a drop of over 10 percent from that category,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>--Rodrigo Martinez/Roger Atwood, Santiago newsroom +56-2-699-5595 x21</p>
